    "updated_at": "2021-10-04T20:15:42Z",
    "body": "Tested against toml-edit `v0.5.0` on the smallest Linode VPS:\r\n\r\n`starship prompt` in an empty directory with full config:\r\n\r\n| Command | Mean [ms] | Min [ms] | Max [ms] | Relative |\r\n|:---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\r\n| `toml` | 4.0 ± 0.4 | 3.4 | 5.7 | 1.00 |\r\n| `toml-edit` | 4.8 ± 0.4 | 4.1 | 7.2 | 1.20 ± 0.17 |\r\n\r\nI pretty consistently see a 20% perf hit on empty repositories when benchmarking.\r\n\r\nThat being said, I'm don't think that relative percent is the correct thing to measure here, since TOML parsing is done once in our program (except for when language modules need to parse toml files). I *think* my one-CPU shared VPS represents a worst-case time increase of 0.8ms on a prompt render (indeed, when running in the starship repository, where git, rust, and package modules need to be run, the difference almost entirely disappears).\r\n\r\nI guess the question I want to ask is \"what's the actual worst-case difference between toml and toml-edit?\" If we're looking at it here, I think it's a no-brainer to make the jump. If there's a case where we could see 20ms added to a 40ms render, I'd have second thoughts.\r\n",
